y by  entire station that is set up but also  social infrastructure like clinics  hospitals all sorts of community  services that make this function like  any real mega city would do ten thousand  five hundred sweepers are employed by  the city it has a government system a  mela Adhikari or the commissioner of the  festival that ensures that land is  allocated the systems for all of this  that the system of the city the mobility  all works efficiently you know it was  the cleanest and the most efficient  Indian city I've lived in and that's  what it looks like in comparison to  Manhattan 30 square kilometres  that's the scale of the city and this is  not an informal city or a pop-up city  this is a formal city this is a state  enterprise the government sets this up  in today's world of neoliberalism and  capitalism where the state has devolved  itself complete responsibility from  making and designing cities this is an  incredible case it's a deliberate  intentional City a formal City and it's  a city that sits on the ground very  likely it sits on the banks of these  rivers and it leaves very little mark  there are no foundations fabric is used  to build this entire city what's also  quite incredible  is that there are five materials that  are used to build this settlement for  seven million people  eight foot tall bamboo string or rope  nails a screw and a skinning material it  could be corrugated metal a fabric or  plastic and these materials come  together and aggregate it's like a kit  of parts and it's used all the way from  a small tent which might house five or  six people or a family to temples that  can house five hundred sometimes a  thousand people and this kit of parts  and this imagination of the city allows  it to be disassembled and so at the end  of the festival within a week the entire  city is disassembled these are again  images from the same spot and the  terrain is offered back to the river as  with the monsoon the water swells again  and it's 